Z||000010||kempo||08-27-2001||08:17 PM||||also, would a sensor cause such a drop in gas mileage?||||reg||1
Z||000011||LarryB||08-27-2001||08:41 PM||||What you're actually seeing is:<P>*_***<BR>*_***<BR>****_****<BR>****_****<P>i.e. code 13 (O2 sensor circuit) and code 44 (lean exhaust).  Rayb had the right answer, check your O2 sensor. Most likely either the wire is disconnected or the sensor corroded and broke into pieces internally.<P>The other tipoff is that after the engine has warmed up you make the light go out immediately by stepping on the gas. <P>The other possibilities are that your throttle position sensor is sticking or you've got a vacuum leak but the O2 sensor is the most likely culprit.  The only one I've had good luck with is the Delco, Ogre likes the Bosch, I wouldn't use anything other than one of these two. <P>And yes, if you're getting codes chances are good your gas mileage will suffer, because codes mean the ECM is getting faulty or incomplete data. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||08-28-2001||11:07 AM||||or look at it this way - the sensors are what tells the the fuel injection system how much gas to squirt into the intake, so if the sensor is disconnected, broken, or worn out then its gonna 'tell' the system to inject too much, or too little fuel.<P>sounds like in your case, too much.||||reg||10
Z||000013||fieroburton||08-28-2001||01:08 PM||||can it make your car stall and quit starting ?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||08-28-2001||02:43 PM||||I could be wrong on this but I think when you first start your car its in an open loop mode - the system looks at engine or air temp, and not much else, and pumps a preset amount of gas through the injectors - then after the car runs for few minutes, if all the sensors are working correctly it goes into closed loop mode where it looks at all the sensors and fine tunes how much gas is injected.<P> So a few failures in the sensors might flood the engine, or not give it enough gas to start. But there are also several other failures that will keep the car from starting too.<P> the three basics for a car that wont start are:<P>1.pull a plug wire and check for spark - maybe pull a plug or two and make sure they are not fouled out. If there is no spark then you have an igntion problem.<P>2. check for gas - if you crank the car for  minute or two and it doesnt fire, the exhaust should smell like raw gas - if not, then you have a fuel injection system problem.<P>3. check for compression - look under the oil filler cap and see if the valves are moving when the engine is cranking - use a compression tester and make sure you have good compression - this test is last cause its very unlikely you will have compression one day, and none the next - unless the timing gear goes out and the cam stops pushing the valves.<P>BTW another casue of O2 sensor codes can be loss of vacuum on some sensors - my car did that - the vacuum hose for the MAP sensor was leaking right under the center of the intake plenium - I had to use a dental mirror and flashlight to see the damaged hose - a mouse had chewed it almost in half. [there were mouse turds in there too]||||reg||1
Z||000015||fldevel||08-28-2001||06:34 PM||||im not sure .. but my rpm will fall below 500 rpm when doing 25 in 4th .. may be coming on due to low RPM also the IAC is probibly wide open when the engine falls that low causing bad gas milage .. try running in a lower gear at low speeds ..sometimes the highest gear isint the best gas milage ||||reg||1
Z||000016||kempo||08-28-2001||09:44 PM||||Woke up this morning, went out to Fiero, and will not idle at all.  Nothing will work unless your on the gas, and runs like **** .  Didn't move it out of the garage.  Don't know if it has any relation to the codes, but I can't drive it.  Replaced the fuel filter, and in a few minutes, the o2 sensor.  Has spark, plugs are good, I know it's getting fuel (it wouldn't even consider running without fuel)...  Any idea's?  Please help!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||10:41 AM||||Replace the O2, disconnect your battery for a few seconds to reset the ECM (Engine Control Module), and then drive the car for 30 miles at moderate speeds (dont lean on the gas pedal)<P>  this will let the ECM re-calibrate itself to the new O2 sensor. Then see how the car runs.||||reg||10
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||10:42 AM||||okay one other thing - since you have been getting poor mileage your car has been running rich - your plugs are probabally fouled out by now =&gt; poor idle.||||reg||1
Z||000019||kempo||08-29-2001||10:09 PM||||OK guys!  This is it...  <BR>I got the new o2 sensor in, new fuel filter, changed plugs, and got her running.  She runs great, best of all - NO MORE CHECK ENGINE LIGHT!  You guys rule!  You have my many thanks for all of the help everybody has given me.  I greatly appreciate it!  <P>-Kemp||||reg||1
